WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.363
President Trump traveled to Utah today,

00:00:02.363 --> 00:00:04.824
where he dramatically cut back the size

00:00:04.824 --> 00:00:07.952
of two national monuments in that state.

00:00:07.952 --> 00:00:10.371
When president
Donald Trump announced plans

00:00:10.371 --> 00:00:13.166
to scale back two national
monuments in Utah,

00:00:13.166 --> 00:00:16.044
there was outcry from a lot of people.

00:00:16.044 --> 00:00:17.712
One company in particular.

00:00:18.629 --> 00:00:20.882
Patagonia really is a resistance brand.

00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:25.136
They embraced the liberal resistance

00:00:25.136 --> 00:00:27.430
against President Donald Trump,

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:31.100
and really put themselves at
the forefront of that movement.

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:35.563
In 2017, it sued the Trump administration

00:00:35.563 --> 00:00:37.398
for sort of slashing the size

00:00:37.398 --> 00:00:39.525
of these two national monuments in Utah

00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:42.362
that were kind of meccas for climbers.

00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:45.531
It said at the time
joining the legal fracas

00:00:45.531 --> 00:00:48.493
was both extraordinary and obvious.

00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:50.119
So the company's general counsel said

00:00:50.119 --> 00:00:53.539
it took exactly one email to the board

00:00:53.539 --> 00:00:54.624
to decide to actually do this,

00:00:54.624 --> 00:00:56.667
and the case is still out.

00:00:56.667 --> 00:00:58.586
Well, I think the only thing

00:00:58.586 --> 00:01:01.839
this administration
understands is lawsuits.

00:01:01.839 --> 00:01:03.091
We're losing this planet,

00:01:03.966 --> 00:01:06.552
and we have an evil government,

00:01:06.552 --> 00:01:09.430
and I'm not gonna stand
back and just let evil win.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.224
This year, it got even more active.

00:01:11.224 --> 00:01:13.351
It changed its mission statement to,

00:01:13.351 --> 00:01:16.854
"Patagonia is in business
to save our home planet."

00:01:17.897 --> 00:01:20.650
And then it manufactured these little tags

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.319
on some of its clothes
that if you flip them up

00:01:23.319 --> 00:01:25.530
it says "vote the assholes out."

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.117
Very implicit running up to the election,

00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:31.202
and it launched a digital tool

00:01:31.202 --> 00:01:33.871
to help people find their polling place,

00:01:33.871 --> 00:01:36.999
and on that digital platform

00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:39.669
it says "vote the climate
deniers out of office"

00:01:39.669 --> 00:01:41.003
again and again on a loop.

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:49.637
This isn't the first time

00:01:49.637 --> 00:01:52.223
that taking a stand has
paid off for Patagonia.

00:01:53.307 --> 00:01:56.185
The business, which is now
worth over a billion dollars,

00:01:56.185 --> 00:01:57.895
was built on it.

00:01:57.895 --> 00:02:00.982
I couldn't care less
about making more money.

00:02:00.982 --> 00:02:02.191
But by doing the right thing,

00:02:02.191 --> 00:02:04.402
it always ends up making me more money.

00:02:07.113 --> 00:02:11.033
So is this all a strategy,

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:13.369
a sort of premeditated strategy?

00:02:13.369 --> 00:02:16.247
Who knows? I mean,
Chouinard's been very vocal

00:02:16.247 --> 00:02:18.875
from the start and makes no bones about,

00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:21.169
he calls himself a dirtbag climber

00:02:21.169 --> 00:02:23.546
and an accidental entrepreneur.

00:02:23.546 --> 00:02:24.839
So I don't think he's fudging this,

00:02:24.839 --> 00:02:26.841
but what's important is that

00:02:26.841 --> 00:02:31.762
it's sort of a textbook marriage
of philanthropy and profit.

00:02:33.306 --> 00:02:35.475
If you wanna understand the entrepreneur,

00:02:35.475 --> 00:02:38.853
study the juvenile delinquent,

00:02:38.853 --> 00:02:40.980
'cause you know, they're saying,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.231
you know, "This sucks,

00:02:45.443 --> 00:02:47.361
and I'm gonna do it my own way."

00:02:54.619 --> 00:02:56.329
Of course, like every other teenage kid,

00:02:56.329 --> 00:02:58.623
I had no idea what I
want to do with my life.

00:03:00.124 --> 00:03:01.417
When I was 16 years old,

00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:03.669
I took off, and drove across the country,

00:03:05.463 --> 00:03:08.341
went into the Wind River Range,
and discovered mountains.

00:03:16.724 --> 00:03:18.684
Patagonia was born out of rock climber

00:03:18.684 --> 00:03:22.021
Yvon Chouinard's first
business, Chouinard Equipment,

00:03:22.021 --> 00:03:24.190
which made and sold climbing supplies.

00:03:25.149 --> 00:03:28.653
Yvon Chouinard was a
blacksmith in the '50s,

00:03:28.653 --> 00:03:30.446
and he started selling climbing gear

00:03:30.446 --> 00:03:31.906
out of the back of his car

00:03:31.906 --> 00:03:34.825
that was less harmful to rock faces.

00:03:34.825 --> 00:03:38.162
This is sort of the origin
story of the company.

00:03:38.162 --> 00:03:40.456
It's called itself the activist company,

00:03:40.456 --> 00:03:42.166
not a activist company.

00:03:45.086 --> 00:03:46.337
Six months out of the year,

00:03:46.337 --> 00:03:48.214
Yvon Chouinard is a blacksmith.

00:03:48.214 --> 00:03:50.132
He makes mountain climbing gear.

00:03:50.132 --> 00:03:51.968
The other half of the year he climbs.

00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:00.268
In 1968, Yvon Chouinard and four friends,

00:04:00.268 --> 00:04:02.853
including Doug Tompkins, the late founder

00:04:02.853 --> 00:04:04.355
of The North Face brand,

00:04:04.355 --> 00:04:07.066
embarked on the ultimate road trip

00:04:07.066 --> 00:04:09.151
from California where they were based

00:04:09.151 --> 00:04:11.862
to Patagonia in Argentina.

00:04:11.862 --> 00:04:13.322
The mountain was in a good mood,

00:04:13.322 --> 00:04:16.158
dazzling sun, calm air all day.

00:04:16.158 --> 00:04:17.159
If this weather holds,

00:04:17.159 --> 00:04:19.161
they could be on the summit within days.

00:04:19.161 --> 00:04:23.082
This trip became a documentary

00:04:23.082 --> 00:04:27.295
about these friends who
were hippies in the '70s

00:04:27.295 --> 00:04:32.174
climbing in Yosemite, where
the sport became a lifestyle,

00:04:32.174 --> 00:04:34.802
and how these friends became pioneer

00:04:34.802 --> 00:04:36.846
of the outdoor industry.

00:04:41.851 --> 00:04:44.812
Chouinard moved from
selling pitons to polyester,

00:04:44.812 --> 00:04:47.106
and in the '70s, Patagonia was born.

00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:52.194
In 1973, Chouinard and
his wife Melinda opened

00:04:52.194 --> 00:04:55.531
the very first Patagonia
store in Ventura, California,

00:04:55.531 --> 00:04:57.825
where the company's
headquarters remain today.

00:04:59.035 --> 00:05:01.120
Melinda Chouinard was the driving force

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.039
behind many of the employee benefits,

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:06.083
like on-site childcare, that
helped the company achieve

00:05:06.083 --> 00:05:09.170
an impressive 4% employee turnover rate.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:11.839
The retail and consumer
product sector average

00:05:11.839 --> 00:05:14.675
is more than triple that at 13%.

00:05:17.678 --> 00:05:20.931
I do think that Patagonia was a leader,

00:05:20.931 --> 00:05:23.225
one of the first corporations

00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:26.520
to take into account, you know,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.982
the human capital, and
make sure that their staff,

00:05:29.982 --> 00:05:32.943
all their employees were
cared for in many ways,

00:05:32.943 --> 00:05:37.782
not just wages, but also they
just cared about their people,

00:05:37.782 --> 00:05:41.118
and knew that that's
a good business model.

00:05:41.118 --> 00:05:43.996
Patagonia began donating
10% of its profits

00:05:43.996 --> 00:05:47.291
to grassroots organizations in 1985.

00:05:47.291 --> 00:05:49.377
This later became the 1% of sales

00:05:49.377 --> 00:05:51.671
that Chouinard labels an earth tax.

00:05:51.671 --> 00:05:53.047
I don't think it's philanthropy.

00:05:53.047 --> 00:05:56.300
It's rent for us living on this planet.

00:05:57.385 --> 00:05:59.595
While Patagonia was creating the blueprint

00:05:59.595 --> 00:06:01.722
of how to be a socially conscious brand,

00:06:01.722 --> 00:06:03.724
it was also turning a profit.

00:06:03.724 --> 00:06:05.518
It even made Inc. magazine's list

00:06:05.518 --> 00:06:08.270
of the fastest growing
privately held companies.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.648
So every time the company makes a decision

00:06:10.648 --> 00:06:13.609
to do something socially,
environmentally active,

00:06:13.609 --> 00:06:17.697
it is met with success, and
that isn't lost on Chouinard,

00:06:17.697 --> 00:06:20.616
or any of the other
leaders of the company.

00:06:20.616 --> 00:06:22.618
The more money Patagonia made,

00:06:22.618 --> 00:06:26.163
the more it gave away under
its philanthropic program.

00:06:26.163 --> 00:06:31.168
Between 1989 and 1991,
the payroll grew by 40%.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:33.754
But trouble was on the horizon.

00:06:33.754 --> 00:06:38.092
I think for 1991 we are
looking at a recession.

00:06:38.092 --> 00:06:40.970
The question is how deep that
recession is going to be,

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.638
and how long we'll be in it.

00:06:42.638 --> 00:06:43.639
Sales fell flat,

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:46.434
and the bank called in the
company's revolving loans.

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:49.437
A company that prided itself
on taking care of its own

00:06:49.437 --> 00:06:53.816
laid off 120 people, roughly
20% of its workforce.

00:06:53.816 --> 00:06:55.776
That was when we decided

00:06:55.776 --> 00:06:57.611
that we were gonna start
doing things differently.

00:06:57.611 --> 00:06:59.488
We were gonna start running the company

00:06:59.488 --> 00:07:02.199
as if it's going to be
here 100 years from now.

00:07:04.452 --> 00:07:06.036
With a renewed sense of purpose,

00:07:06.036 --> 00:07:09.498
Patagonia was ready for what
it called business unusual.

00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:12.001
It started with the costly move

00:07:12.001 --> 00:07:14.378
to recycled and organic materials

00:07:14.378 --> 00:07:16.756
before launching a clothing repair service

00:07:16.756 --> 00:07:18.466
to discourage fast fashion,

00:07:19.383 --> 00:07:22.011
and Patagonia's influence was growing.

00:07:22.011 --> 00:07:24.764
What was intended as Chouinard's
philosophical handbook

00:07:24.764 --> 00:07:27.475
for employees became an
international blueprint

00:07:27.475 --> 00:07:30.603
for sustainable business
printed in 10 languages.

00:07:32.938 --> 00:07:35.524
What's interesting to me is that all this,

00:07:35.524 --> 00:07:37.985
I hate the term on brand,
but it's very on brand.

00:07:37.985 --> 00:07:40.946
It really works for the kind
of company he's created.

00:07:40.946 --> 00:07:42.823
It works for the kind of employees

00:07:42.823 --> 00:07:45.659
he's been able to recruit and retain.

00:07:45.659 --> 00:07:49.872
It all, it is very seamless
in terms of the identity

00:07:49.872 --> 00:07:54.877
of him and the company and what
it means in the marketplace.

00:07:55.294 --> 00:07:57.797
So over time, the company's mission

00:07:57.797 --> 00:08:00.758
and its marketing have
become the same thing.

00:08:00.758 --> 00:08:04.053
The inflection point came in 2011,

00:08:04.053 --> 00:08:05.471
actually Black Friday.

00:08:05.471 --> 00:08:09.266
Patagonia took out a huge
ad in The New York Times

00:08:09.266 --> 00:08:11.977
that said "don't buy this jacket,"

00:08:11.977 --> 00:08:15.981
and it sort of got into the
nitty gritty on, you know,

00:08:15.981 --> 00:08:18.901
the carbon footprint
and the water footprint

00:08:18.901 --> 00:08:22.780
that goes into, you know, a
piece of apparel like a parka,

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:24.532
and at the time Chouinard said

00:08:24.532 --> 00:08:28.536
the best jacket for our planet
is one that already exists.

00:08:28.536 --> 00:08:31.497
So the company at that time
said it needed to address

00:08:31.497 --> 00:08:33.332
the issue of consumerism,

00:08:33.332 --> 00:08:36.460
which coming from a for-profit business

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:37.962
hits pretty hypocritical.

00:08:40.089 --> 00:08:41.924
As the company encouraged its customers

00:08:41.924 --> 00:08:46.303
to buy less, its annual sales
increased by almost 40%.

00:08:46.303 --> 00:08:49.348
The stance of this is
hypocritical was loud,

00:08:49.348 --> 00:08:52.977
and constant talking about
consumerism in a critical light

00:08:52.977 --> 00:08:55.396
while very much being a part of it.

00:08:56.647 --> 00:09:00.192
Fast fashion is top on the list

00:09:00.192 --> 00:09:02.444
of environmental destruction,

00:09:02.444 --> 00:09:04.905
and not just destroying the environment,

00:09:04.905 --> 00:09:07.241
but impacting human health.

00:09:07.241 --> 00:09:09.326
They are in the fashion industry.

00:09:09.326 --> 00:09:11.036
They're a clothing retailer,

00:09:11.036 --> 00:09:16.041
and to come head-to-head
with that is courageous.

00:09:16.083 --> 00:09:19.920
You're not only just
trying to create a product

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.756
that'll last, but you're also educating

00:09:22.756 --> 00:09:27.511
an entire generation
of people to understand

00:09:27.511 --> 00:09:30.139
that whole ideology that we don't need

00:09:30.139 --> 00:09:32.349
to change out our outfits.

00:09:33.392 --> 00:09:37.146
In 2014, Rose Marcario
was appointed as CEO.

00:09:37.146 --> 00:09:39.398
During the next six
years, she grew Patagonia

00:09:39.398 --> 00:09:41.191
into a billion dollar company,

00:09:41.191 --> 00:09:43.944
while also scaling up
its environmental goals,

00:09:43.944 --> 00:09:47.072
and bringing a more political
voice to its activism.

00:09:47.072 --> 00:09:50.534
Yvon had this model where
he was basically saying

00:09:50.534 --> 00:09:54.330
you can have a great business,
you can make quality product,

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.958
but you can also do the right thing

00:09:57.958 --> 00:10:01.211
by the environment, by your
employees, by your community,

00:10:01.211 --> 00:10:04.757
and that to me was the
most holistic vision

00:10:04.757 --> 00:10:06.884
of business that I'd ever seen.

00:10:06.884 --> 00:10:10.387
So right now, Patagonia has
a team of about 20 people

00:10:10.387 --> 00:10:13.015
solely focused on activism and grants,

00:10:13.015 --> 00:10:15.726
and this all comes at a
time in the past decade

00:10:15.726 --> 00:10:18.562
when consumers are paying
much more attention

00:10:18.562 --> 00:10:20.940
to the social footprint of companies

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:22.900
and where they buy their goods.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.819
So what's happened is all this is

00:10:25.819 --> 00:10:27.112
good business for Patagonia.

00:10:27.112 --> 00:10:30.157
In the past 10 years, revenue
and profit at the company

00:10:30.157 --> 00:10:31.825
have roughly quadrupled.

00:10:31.825 --> 00:10:34.244
It's private, but the
estimates are it does

00:10:34.244 --> 00:10:38.082
about a billion dollars in
annual sales, which is sizable,

00:10:38.082 --> 00:10:39.583
and that's part of the reason why

00:10:39.583 --> 00:10:42.586
it's been such a fortuitous cycle.

00:10:42.586 --> 00:10:44.922
As it's ramped up its activism,

00:10:44.922 --> 00:10:48.050
and become more vocal on these things,

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:49.635
it's resonated with the market.

00:10:51.011 --> 00:10:53.555
As the company branched
out into other areas,

00:10:53.555 --> 00:10:56.934
including a venture capital
fund for environmental causes,

00:10:56.934 --> 00:11:00.187
and sustainable food,
revenue reached a new peak

00:11:00.187 --> 00:11:03.399
of over $1 billion in 2017.

00:11:03.399 --> 00:11:05.401
But this was still small change compared

00:11:05.401 --> 00:11:07.861
to fast fashion heavyweights like Nike

00:11:07.861 --> 00:11:10.572
who made $37 billion that same year.

00:11:13.033 --> 00:11:15.828
Patagonia's conscience
hasn't always been reflected

00:11:15.828 --> 00:11:17.454
in its customer base either.

00:11:19.581 --> 00:11:23.293
Personalized vests have become
the go-to corporate uniform

00:11:23.293 --> 00:11:25.295
and the expensive price
tag has helped the brand

00:11:25.295 --> 00:11:27.089
earn the nickname Patagucci.

00:11:28.048 --> 00:11:30.926
It's expensive stuff,
it's expensive apparel,

00:11:30.926 --> 00:11:35.180
and that is part of their
calculus in terms of the activism.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:38.142
You know, a lot of the
more sustainable parts

00:11:38.142 --> 00:11:39.977
of the supply chain, there's a premium

00:11:39.977 --> 00:11:42.563
for organic cotton, or sustainable down.

00:11:42.563 --> 00:11:45.899
The Patagucci label is a symptom of that.

00:11:45.899 --> 00:11:48.986
So Patagonia products are very popular

00:11:48.986 --> 00:11:51.864
of course with people who
are active in the outdoors,

00:11:51.864 --> 00:11:54.658
but also with people are not as active.

00:11:54.658 --> 00:11:57.119
For instance, you know, people in finance

00:11:57.119 --> 00:11:59.621
wear the Patagonia vest
to go to the office

00:11:59.621 --> 00:12:04.626
and that's because of the
image that the brand projects.

00:12:04.752 --> 00:12:07.546
And so, they asked to live with the fact

00:12:07.546 --> 00:12:10.883
although they, you know,
preach for the environment

00:12:10.883 --> 00:12:12.843
and want to stand for good,

00:12:12.843 --> 00:12:15.345
they're victims of their own success.

00:12:16.638 --> 00:12:18.265
Patagonia set sales records

00:12:18.265 --> 00:12:21.852
in the first three years of
the Trump administration.

00:12:21.852 --> 00:12:25.147
Now in 2020, things have changed.

00:12:25.147 --> 00:12:28.025
As COVID-19 brought the
business world to a halt,

00:12:28.025 --> 00:12:31.403
and the American people voted
in a new administration,

00:12:31.403 --> 00:12:33.822
Patagonia appointed Ryan Gellert as CEO.

00:12:36.241 --> 00:12:38.202
As far as its CEO transition goes,

00:12:38.202 --> 00:12:40.621
this one's happening quite strangely.

00:12:40.621 --> 00:12:43.165
There's no real budget for next year yet,

00:12:43.165 --> 00:12:45.125
because of everything that's going on,

00:12:45.125 --> 00:12:47.961
and all the uncertainty around that.

00:12:50.798 --> 00:12:54.384
Patagonia's new focus is
going to be on forging

00:12:54.384 --> 00:12:58.722
a relationship with the
Biden administration.

00:12:58.722 --> 00:13:02.810
It should be a more close relationship,

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.896
but Gellert still has his guard up.

00:13:05.896 --> 00:13:10.234
During the Obama administration,
they didn't always agree,

00:13:10.234 --> 00:13:13.570
and he expects the same with
Biden in the White House.

00:13:16.448 --> 00:13:19.409
They plan to bring
litigation wherever needed

00:13:19.409 --> 00:13:21.870
against any government needed

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:23.664
about the issues that they care about

00:13:23.664 --> 00:13:25.874
and that's climate change,
and the environment.

